Waits on the POSIX (macOS) emulation of guest kernel objects, on top of rexglue-upstream-posix-fixes.patch (2026-11-04):

  #501 perf(core): WaitMultiple sleeps until a handle is signalled instead of polling
       (ported: it conflicts with rexglue-linux-wait-sleep.patch and #531; the remaining time
       stays exact, so a wait under 1 ms still does not spin)
  #531, two corrections to the version taken in rexglue-upstream-posix-fixes.patch:
       - SignalAndWait signalled the other object while holding the waited object's lock, so
         SignalAndWait(A, B) and SignalAndWait(B, A) on two threads could deadlock. The waiter
         now takes its ticket first, then signals with its lock released: the release is still
         reserved for it, which is the guarantee the PR is after.
       - When a reserved auto-reset release folded back into a plain signal (its eligible waiter
         left), nobody was woken: a waiter that registered after the reservation slept on, until
         its timeout or for good.

Tests: tests/unit/core/wait_multiple_test.cpp (from #501), tests/unit/core/event_handoff_test.cpp.

